> Derive the greedy rule and its geometric objective

For vertices p[i]=(x[i],y[i]), the complete graph has
M=n(n-1)/2 edges, with weights

\[
w_{ij}=\operatorname{hypot}(x_i-x_j,y_i-y_j),\quad i<j,
\qquad \min_T\sum_{(i,j)\in T}w_{ij}.
\]

Kruskal scans these edges in nondecreasing weight order. It accepts an
edge exactly when its endpoints belong to different current components.
An accepted edge reduces the number of components by one without
creating a cycle. Starting from n components, n-1 accepted edges give
a connected acyclic spanning tree when n>0. The empty graph is
connected by this API's convention; a singleton needs no edges.

Why is each greedy acceptance safe? Maintain a minimum tree containing
the already accepted forest. If the next edge e is absent, insert it
into that tree: a cycle appears. That cycle contains an edge h crossing
the current component cut. Such an edge cannot be lighter than e;
an earlier lighter crossing edge would already have joined components.
Replacing h by e therefore preserves a minimum tree containing the new
forest. Ties permit this exchange, so the lightest cut edge belongs to
some minimum tree, rather than necessarily every minimum tree.

The generic kernel uses path halving and union by rank. Its trace
records the actual roots before each decision and the actual number
of components afterwards. Roots are implementation representatives,
not sorted component labels. Point indices and endpoint orientations
remain attached to their original records throughout sorting.

> A short cycle is rejected before a distant point joins

Place three points near the origin and a fourth at (10,0). The first
two unit edges join the cluster. Its remaining diagonal has length
sqrt(2), but now closes a cycle, so it is rejected. The next edge of
length nine joins the distant point. This is an actual rejected scan,
not an edge inferred from the final tree after execution.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euclidean_mst_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto t=dm::algorithms::euclidean_minimum_spanning_tree_trace({{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{10,0}});const auto& k=t.kruskal;
  std::print("Cluster: total {}; accepted {}; rejected cycles {}; scanned {}; all pair distances {}.\n\n",k.outcome.result.total_weight,k.outcome.work.accepted_edges,k.outcome.work.cycle_rejections,k.outcome.work.scanned_edges,t.distance_evaluations);
  std::print("| scan | edge | weight | roots before | decision | components after |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke accepted;accepted.color={37,99,235};accepted.width=4;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke rejected;rejected.color={220,38,38};rejected.width=4;rejected.dashed=true;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<k.steps.size();++i){const auto& step=k.steps[i];auto [u,v,w]=k.input_edges.at(step.input_index);std::print("| {} | {}-{} | {:.9f} | {},{} | {} | {} |\n",i,u,v,w,step.root_u,step.root_v,step.accepted?"accept":"reject cycle",step.components_after);
    d.path({{t.points[u].x,t.points[u].y},{t.points[v].x,t.points[v].y}},step.accepted?accepted:rejected);}
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.circle=true;node.width=.25;node.height=.25;node.font_size=22;node.padding=6;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.points.size();++i)d.node(std::to_string(i),{t.points[i].x,t.points[i].y},std::to_string(i),node);
  d.text({5,2.1},"A cluster cycle is rejected before the long link",26).text({5,-1.35},"Blue accepted; dashed red rejected; actual coordinates",22).save_svg("build/euclidean_mst_cycle.svg");
}
