\caption(Both plots contain five labeled input records but four visible positions. The actual returned zero edge links the duplicate records; scaling triples every selected weight and the total.)
\alttext(Two coordinate plots show the same selected square-side geometry before and after scaling and translation. Duplicate records overlap at one plotted position; their zero edge is preserved in the computed table.)

An MST minimizes total length over edges joining the given vertices.
It does not form a tour visiting every point in cyclic order, minimize
root-to-point path lengths, or introduce Steiner junctions. Comparing
squared distances preserves individual edge ordering in exact arithmetic,
but summing squared selected weights is a different objective from the
Euclidean length reported here. The core uses hypot lengths throughout.
These coordinates are Cartesian units, not latitude/longitude distances.

> Every pair is generated even when scanning finishes early

The implementation builds the full M-edge graph, sorts a stable index
permutation, then performs union-find scans. With M=n(n-1)/2, this costs
O(n^2 log(n+1)) time and O(n^2+n) runtime storage. Early tree completion
can save scans, but does not save pair-distance generation or the sort.
No Delaunay sparsification is implemented by this API.

For n equally spaced collinear points at coordinates 0,...,n-1, all
n-1 adjacent unit edges come first and connect the line. The exact
minimum is n-1: its extremes are distance n-1 apart, and a spanning
tree's path between them cannot have smaller total length. This
family therefore gives M actual distance evaluations, S=n-1 actual
scans, and optional capture C=n+M+S=M+2n-1. Input sizes and outputs are
logical counts, not elapsed-time benchmarks or peak-memory estimates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euclidean_mst_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::vector<double> sizes,pairs,scans,capture;
  std::print("| vertices n | pair distances M | scanned edges S | accepted edges | tree length | captured records C |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t n:{2,4,8,16,32,64,128}){std::vector<dm::algorithms::Point2D> p;for(std::size_t i=0;i<n;++i)p.push_back({double(i),0});const auto t=dm::algorithms::euclidean_minimum_spanning_tree_trace(p);const auto& w=t.kruskal.outcome.work;
    if(t.distance_evaluations!=n*(n-1)/2||w.scanned_edges!=n-1||t.kruskal.outcome.result.total_weight!=double(n-1)||t.captured_entries!=n*(n-1)/2+2*n-1)throw std::runtime_error("known line family failed");
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,t.distance_evaluations,w.scanned_edges,w.accepted_edges,t.kruskal.outcome.result.total_weight,t.captured_entries);sizes.push_back(n);pairs.push_back(std::log2(t.distance_evaluations));scans.push_back(std::log2(w.scanned_edges));capture.push_back(std::log2(t.captured_entries));}
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.line(sizes,pairs,"pairs M",{37,99,235}).points(sizes,pairs,"",{37,99,235},5).line(sizes,scans,"actual scans S",{220,38,38}).points(sizes,scans,"",{220,38,38},5).title("All distances precede early tree completion").x_label("Number of line vertices n").y_label("log2(logical pair or scan count)");
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.line(sizes,capture,"C=n+M+S",{124,58,237}).points(sizes,capture,"",{124,58,237},5).title("Optional trace retains inputs and scans").x_label("Number of line vertices n").y_label("log2(logical retained records)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,520).title("Known line optima: complete-pair work remains quadratic").fit_panels().save_svg("build/euclidean_mst_work.svg");
}
```
