\caption(The exact eight-record triangle trace succeeds, while both insufficient quotas fail. All shown invalid-domain and arithmetic cases throw explicit exceptions. Record counts deliberately exclude runtime and output storage.)
\alttext(A native three-box diagram shows three point records, three complete edge records and two actual scan records, summing to eight logical captured records.)

The native tests preserve 306 frozen valid legacy outputs, including
stable ties and generic signed-weight forests, and check actual scan
certificates, 350 random planar fixtures, 30 exhaustive small-tree
comparisons, 400 generic graphs, duplicate vertices, coordinate
transformations, exact work families, ownership and quota boundaries.
Full finite validation intentionally changes old nonfinite behavior.
The original square remains a three-edge tree of total length three.

> Reference

J. B. Kruskal, Jr. (1956), On the shortest spanning subtree of a graph
and the traveling salesman problem, Proceedings of the American
Mathematical Society 7(1), 48-50,
https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9939-1956-0078686-7.
The greedy forest construction is applied here to a complete graph of
planar Euclidean distances. Stable input-order ties, checked finite
arithmetic and bounded actual scan histories are implementation choices.
